// Navigational judgments: one query, one correct URL.
//
// Different in kind from judgments.jsonl next door. That file grades many
// URLs per query 0-3 for NDCG, because "which page best answers this?" has
// degrees of rightness. A navigational query does not: the user is trying to
// reach one specific page, and everything else is wrong. So the metric is
// success@1 — did the exact URL come back first — and the judgment is a
// single URL.
//
// Matching is canonical (common::canonical_url), so http/https, a leading
// www. and a trailing slash do not count against an answer.
//
// Honesty notes on how this set was built, because they bound what the
// number means:
//   - Every gold URL was fetched and confirmed live before being written
//     here, independently of the resolver.
//   - Entities were chosen first and their URLs looked up second, so the set
//     is not a list of things the resolver already handles. Several cases
//     are here because they are expected to FAIL: a set with no failures
//     measures nothing.
//   - The known bias: these are institutions whose URLs the author could
//     verify, which skews toward sites that have one. A harder set would be
//     drawn from query logs.

